1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
How do we show them?

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:06,040
We just put a parenthesis, and then we list payoffs in 10-3.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:09,200
And player one gets the first payoff.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,760
Player two gets the second payoff.

5
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:13,440
It's very normal in game theory to just call people player one,

6
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:15,040
player two, player three.

7
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,840
Normally, player one is the person who plays first.

8
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:19,600
Player two is the player who plays second.

9
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:21,400
Player three is the player who plays third.

10
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,280
So it's very simple.

11
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:26,240
OK.

12
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:30,400
All right, so you could have your career game tree.

13
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,200
So this could be a really boring one-player game, where

14
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,680
you start off and you decide, do I

15
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:38,520
want to either drop out of high school or finish?

16
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:40,920
And then once you finish high school, you could say, OK,

17
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:45,960
now do I want to go on to college or not?

18
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:48,760
And then the other one, do I want to finish college or not?

19
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:51,160
And then do I want to go and get a master's degree or not,

20
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:54,080
get a PhD or not, get a second PhD or not?

21
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,240
And you could show that branching out like one

22
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:58,840
of these upside-down trees.

23
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:00,840
Yes, because these game trees, by the way,

24
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:04,440
are always drawn upside-down.

25
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:04,920
Why?

26
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:07,440
Well, practice, it's because it's

27
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:09,440
normal to go and read from the top of the page

28
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:11,880
to the bottom of the page when you're reading English

29
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:13,520
or anywhere, or most Western.

30
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:15,480
It's all Western languages, anyway.

31
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:16,880
It's worked that way.

32
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:17,880
OK.

33
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:18,880
All right.

34
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:19,380
OK.

35
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:20,600
So we have a simple example.

36
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,040
A more interesting one would be something

37
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,480
based upon an old and pretty boring movie called

38
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,000
Trench Connection, Gene Hackman in the 70s.

39
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:28,880
You might like it.

40
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:29,460
I don't know.

41
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:30,760
It's one I saw as an adult.

42
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:32,260
This is what they watched back then.

43
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:33,880
It's a good job.

44
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:38,240
Anyway, so there's a cop who's strangely named Popeye.

45
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:39,200
And I don't know why.

46
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:40,520
He's not a cartoon character.

47
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:42,640
He does not like the spinach-eating cartoon

48
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:44,200
character.

49
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,000
Both his eyes are fine.

50
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,680
But anyway, he's called Popeye.

51
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,800
And he is pursuing a criminal onto a New York subway.

52
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,360
And when this criminal notices Popeye,

53
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:57,120
Popeye notices the criminal.

54
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:59,560
And then they're trying to figure out what to do.

55
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,760
Because Popeye, in order to follow the criminal,

56
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:04,280
needs to do the same thing as the criminal.

57
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:06,920
If the criminal's on the subway, Popeye must be on the subway.

58
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,400
If the criminal's not on the subway,

59
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:10,520
Popeye must not be on the subway.

60
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:12,080
On the other hand, the criminal wants

61
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:14,360
to do the opposite of what the cop who's following him

62
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:15,000
is doing.

63
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,840
If the cop is on the subway, the criminal wants to get off.

64
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:20,600
And if the cop is not on the subway,

65
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:23,560
the criminal wants to be on.

66
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:26,880
So what you could do is start off with, first of all,

67
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,520
the criminal, player one, decides

68
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:30,400
whether to be on or off.

69
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,880
And then Popeye comes along.

70
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:34,240
And he decides whether he wants to be on or off.

71
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:41,800
And then we could go and list the following payoffs.

72
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,360
So say that if you get what you want,

73
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:45,840
then you have a payoff of 10.

74
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:47,640
If you don't, you have a payoff of 0.

75
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:49,720
So if they're both on, then the criminal

76
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:50,880
went first as a payoff of 0.

77
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,120
And Popeye is 10.

78
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:57,080
And if the criminal is on and Popeye is off,

79
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:58,360
then the criminal gets 10.

80
00:02:58,360 --> 00:02:59,760
Popeye gets 0.

81
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:02,280
If it's off-on, then you get 10, 0.

82
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:05,240
Then finally, off-off would be 0, 10.

83
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:10,440
And that is the extensive form for that game.

84
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,480
Now, there's a few different complications

85
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:15,240
that get worked into this approach.

86
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:18,680
So one of them is, what if nature is playing?

87
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:19,840
What if nature is playing?

88
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:23,800
What if it can either be icy or not be icy?

89
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:25,720
And the normal way of doing this is actually

90
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:28,800
to just insert nature into the game

91
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:32,240
as a player who randomizes.

92
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:33,440
And why are they randomized?

93
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:35,120
Well, intuitively, you just think nature

94
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:37,040
gets the same payoff regardless.

95
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:39,720
Because nature isn't different to our existence.

96
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:42,280
Nature does not care about human affairs.

97
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:44,200
And therefore, nature gets modeled

98
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:47,840
as a player that acts randomly.

99
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:51,840
So there are a lot of models where someone takes an action,

100
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,840
and then nature plays.

101
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:57,520
Nature could be, for example, there's either a hard earther,

102
00:03:57,520 --> 00:03:59,280
there's rainstorms or not.

103
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:01,600
And then this determines whether the farmer invested well

104
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:02,100
or poorly.

105
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:06,440
Now, another thing you may be wondering about

106
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:09,000
is, how does information work in this game?

107
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,600
And the key insight here is that when you really think about it,

108
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:18,280
there's no difference at all between moving simultaneously

109
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:20,760
and moving sequentially but not knowing what

110
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:23,120
your opponent did first.

111
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:26,000
So for example, suppose that we could play rock, paper, scissors.

112
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,040
We either do it simultaneously, which is the usual way,

113
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:32,360
or I could go and write what I'm doing on a piece of paper

114
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:36,440
secretly, put it down, walk away so I can't touch it anymore,

115
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:39,120
and then you write down what you're going to do secretly

116
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:42,360
so you don't get to see what I did

117
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:45,720
and I can't change what I do, and then we open them.

118
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:48,560
And what's the difference between doing it simultaneously

119
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:51,160
with full information or doing it sequentially

120
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:52,520
without the information?

121
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:54,880
The answer is it's exactly the same thing.

122
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,840
And that's nice because to do it as a tree,

123
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:00,320
everything needs to be sequential.

124
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:05,200
So anyway, we can go and model this,

125
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:08,480
what appears to be simultaneity here.

126
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:10,120
And we can make everything sequential.

127
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:15,680
And what we do is that if, say, let's see,

128
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:20,160
if this player doesn't get to see what happened there,

129
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:22,640
then we just brought dots around that first thing.

130
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:32,000
So basically, you put dots around there,

131
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,120
and that shows that when player two is deciding,

132
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:36,920
he doesn't know where he is in the tree.

133
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:39,920
So we're still thinking of it as sequential,

134
00:05:39,920 --> 00:05:41,360
and then you might say, well, yeah,

135
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:43,880
couldn't we just as well flip it around and change who player

136
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:44,880
one and player two are?

137
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:45,760
Yeah, that's true.

138
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:48,400
But still, this means that we can go and model

139
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:50,600
this in this nice tree shape.

140
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:55,760
So if encyclopedias moves are equivalent sequentially,

141
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:57,880
we're presenting potential moves with uncertainty.

142
00:05:57,880 --> 00:05:59,300
And then, again, the whole idea is

143
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:01,440
if you learn something before you decide,

144
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:04,600
then the node representing what's learned must precede

145
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:08,320
the node the decision is taken.

146
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:10,000
So this is extensive forms.

147
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,720
Extensive forms are mainly used for very simple games

148
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,880
because it's really hard to draw this

149
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:16,600
when the game is really big.

150
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:19,000
And it's very hard to draw it if there's a lot of players.

151
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:23,840
But there are people who've gotten Nobel prizes

152
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:25,360
for pretty simple models.

153
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:29,920
So this can be done, say, on Reinhardt's Elm

154
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:31,240
with the chain store model.

155
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:34,480
That's a fairly simple model, but barely more complicated

156
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:37,280
than this.

157
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:39,960
Questions about extensive forms?

158
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:41,440
Have you assessed forms yet?

159
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:42,920
Under what conditions can we assume

160
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:45,880
that everybody knows all the payoffs,

161
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:48,280
no matter what decisions are made after their decisions?

162
00:06:48,280 --> 00:06:49,760
So that's a great question.

163
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:52,080
And the game theory answer is, well,

164
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:53,520
if people don't know the payoffs,

165
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:55,120
then we just have to add more levels

166
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:57,080
of complexity to the game.

167
00:06:57,080 --> 00:06:58,520
You say, man, but people can't even

168
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:01,560
understand the simple version of the game.

169
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:03,840
So that is the idea, is that if people don't know what

170
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:06,600
the payoffs are, then you just add an extra level saying,

171
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:07,920
well, then they think the payoffs

172
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:09,760
could be a bunch of different possibilities.

173
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:11,040
We just have to make it bigger.

174
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:12,800
So that's how you would conventionally

175
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,800
model any kind of confusion.

176
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:17,400
And actually, there is a whole set of models

177
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:19,480
that people use on the idea, well,

178
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:21,600
maybe people are cooperating with Persona of Silence

179
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:23,880
because they don't understand the payoffs.

180
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:25,680
And so that's very similar, actually,

181
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:28,400
to that self-managed chain store paradox I was mentioning.

182
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:32,800
So there are models like that, I guess.

183
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:37,240
So if you have a sequential game, and we let, for example,

184
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:39,880
like the second player, the player two,

185
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:43,640
assume that in each case the player one made,

186
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:46,960
what the second player will choose, at the same time,

187
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:49,640
is that still a sequential game or not?

188
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:50,140
Let's see.

189
00:07:50,140 --> 00:07:52,600
So you're saying, so the second player

190
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:54,320
does know what the first player did?

191
00:07:54,320 --> 00:07:56,440
He doesn't know, but he assumed that in this case,

192
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:57,360
what he would choose.

193
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:00,360
And if the player, if we assume that the first player chose

194
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:02,080
on, then I would decide.

195
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:03,880
So player two just makes an assumption

196
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:05,240
about what the player one did?

197
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:06,840
Yeah, they would.

198
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:09,880
So I'd say, so then we're probably

199
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:11,440
in the realm of psychology, which

200
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:13,920
is great, and we'll talk about it after midterm.

201
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:17,840
But if people make an assumption without any,

202
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:20,400
people just assume that people are doing A,

203
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:21,920
when actually, some time, actually,

204
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:23,520
they're not doing A 100% of the time,

205
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:25,800
then that's a systematic bias.

206
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:29,560
And then we need psychology to go and analyze that.

207
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:32,760
I mean, assume that the first player chose on,

208
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:35,720
then the second player chose on or off.

209
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:38,320
And then assume that the first player chose off,

210
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:40,240
then the second player chose on or off.

211
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,120
And then they will reveal the true decision.

212
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:45,080
OK, so that's when they don't know what the other one did.

213
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:45,800
Yeah, they know.

214
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:48,440
But if the real decision is revealed,

215
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:53,320
they will pay by the real decision.

216
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:55,400
Possibly, we're going to answer your question when

217
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:56,600
we do an abstract equilibrium.

218
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:58,520
How about after we do an abstract equilibrium,

219
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:01,480
can you tell me if you're happy or if you're still?

220
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:01,980
No.

221
00:09:01,980 --> 00:09:02,480
No.

222
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:02,980
OK.

223
00:09:02,980 --> 00:09:03,720
Yes, that's fine.

224
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:04,220
No, no, no.

225
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:07,480
It's a good question.

226
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:09,920
OK, so anyway, so since it gets hard

227
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:12,360
to draw these extensive forms, and they're generally

228
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:15,400
only used for simpler problems, although simple

229
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:18,560
doesn't mean trivial, you can get a Nobel Prize

230
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:20,560
for simple problems.

231
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:24,280
You do it well, and a lot of people say you.

232
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:26,920
But more commonly, we do normal forms,

233
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:29,240
which are also called strategic forms.

234
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:31,760
So what's going on with normal forms

235
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:35,960
is you get a complete grid of strategy profiles and payoffs.

236
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:38,440
And the grid is one dimension per player.

237
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:39,920
Right now, of course, that's also

238
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:40,880
getting a little complicated.

239
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:41,760
What if there's four players?

240
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:43,240
We need a four-dimensional diagram.

241
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:45,480
But you may notice, wow, there's not that many theory

242
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:48,760
papers with four players.

243
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:51,480
Is that because the world never has four people interacting?

244
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:55,080
Or is it because it's too hard to go and draw

245
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:56,880
a four-dimensional diagram?

246
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:57,760
Interesting question.

247
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:01,320
I won't resolve it here, but it's suspicious.

248
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:04,560
OK, now an interesting thing about these normal forms

249
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:06,440
that we're going to see is that there's often

250
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:09,240
going to be irrelevant information contained

251
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:10,920
in the grid.

252
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:13,320
Why put in irrelevant information?

253
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:15,840
Because mathematicians are into this stuff,

254
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:18,200
and mathematicians love completeness.

255
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,920
Mathematicians like to cover every possibility.

256
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:22,760
It's like, what would we do if we randomly

257
00:10:22,760 --> 00:10:24,160
pick an irrational number?

258
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:25,680
Don't we have a proof that only that happens

259
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:26,760
with zero probability?

260
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:29,800
Yes, but what's our contingency plan for this event?

261
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:32,240
All right, so for the zero probability event,

262
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:34,080
I still need to know what we would

263
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:37,000
do if the thing that never happens happened.

264
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,440
And you may say, who cares?

265
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:40,400
